<linker>
	<assembly fullname="UnityEngine">
		<type fullname="UnityEngine.Animator" preserve="all" />
		<type fullname="UnityEngine.MeshRenderer" preserve="all" />
		<type fullname="UnityEngine.SkinnedMeshRenderer" preserve="all" />
		<type fullname="UnityEngine.MeshFilter" preserve="all" />
		<type fullname="UnityEngine.SpriteRenderer" preserve="all" />
	</assembly>

	<!--
		The teacher PIN is a salted SHA-256 hash, and SHA256.Create() resolves its
		implementation through CryptoConfig by REFLECTION. Android ships IL2CPP with managed
		stripping on, and nothing here preserved the crypto assemblies — so the linker was free
		to remove the very type that call returns.

		The cost of being wrong is not a cosmetic failure. TeacherGate.Hash is behind every
		adult action on the tablet: setting the PIN at install, unlocking the next session, and
		EXPORTING THE LOGS. A throw inside a UI callback is swallowed and logged by Unity, so
		the teacher would tap Export and see nothing happen, on the one control that retrieves
		the entire dataset — and there is no second chance to collect it after the study.

		Invisible in the Editor, which does not strip. Preserved rather than diagnosed after the
		fact, because the first APK is also the study build.
	-->
	<assembly fullname="System.Security.Cryptography.Algorithms" preserve="all" />
	<assembly fullname="System.Security.Cryptography.Primitives" preserve="all" />
</linker>
